WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:11.210 --> 00:00:14.030
ANNAH MOYO: My name
is Annah Yvonne Moyo.

00:00:14.030 --> 00:00:17.060
I am currently acting
executive director

00:00:17.060 --> 00:00:20.480
at the Center for the Study of
Violence and Reconciliation.

00:00:20.480 --> 00:00:24.560
CSVR in short based in
Johannesburg, South Africa.

00:00:24.560 --> 00:00:26.810
I am a transitional
justice expert

00:00:26.810 --> 00:00:29.990
and have worked extensively
on the African continent

00:00:29.990 --> 00:00:35.900
on transitional justice issues
from addressing colonial era

00:00:35.900 --> 00:00:38.240
legacies through
transitional justice,

00:00:38.240 --> 00:00:41.840
addressing economic issues
through transitional justice,

00:00:41.840 --> 00:00:44.180
but also addressing
some of the current

00:00:44.180 --> 00:00:47.780
and emerging challenges
and issues in practice

00:00:47.780 --> 00:00:49.118
through transitional justice.

00:00:49.118 --> 00:00:51.410
LILIANE UMUBYEYI: Thank you
so much for taking the time

00:00:51.410 --> 00:00:55.250
today to discuss with us.

00:00:55.250 --> 00:01:00.980
Maybe before we start
with the main topic,

00:01:00.980 --> 00:01:04.427
could you tell us what
is transitional justice?

00:01:04.427 --> 00:01:06.260
I'm sure there are a
lot of people who don't

00:01:06.260 --> 00:01:09.440
know what this term means.

00:01:09.440 --> 00:01:11.660
ANNAH MOYO: Transitional
justice basically

00:01:11.660 --> 00:01:18.380
refers to a set of measures
that include truth, reparations,

00:01:18.380 --> 00:01:23.510
justice, and accountability and
guarantees of non recurrence

00:01:23.510 --> 00:01:26.360
or conflict that are
aimed at addressing

00:01:26.360 --> 00:01:29.000
the consequences of
conflict, consequences

00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:32.360
of authoritarian rule,
and other incidents that

00:01:32.360 --> 00:01:35.480
bring about gross
human rights violations

00:01:35.480 --> 00:01:37.970
and other human
rights violations

00:01:37.970 --> 00:01:41.930
and crimes in a particular
setting, be it a society,

00:01:41.930 --> 00:01:45.170
a nation, a country,
or a region.

00:01:45.170 --> 00:01:49.130
That is the simple definition
of transitional justice.

00:01:49.130 --> 00:01:51.120
On the African
continent, for instance,

00:01:51.120 --> 00:01:55.130
we have expanded this definition
to really also include

00:01:55.130 --> 00:01:57.470
specific tenets
of transformation

00:01:57.470 --> 00:01:59.940
or transformative
justice, so to speak.

00:01:59.940 --> 00:02:02.970
So we are looking
at these measures,

00:02:02.970 --> 00:02:08.150
including formal and non-formal
measures or mechanisms

00:02:08.150 --> 00:02:10.759
also including traditional
justice mechanisms

00:02:10.759 --> 00:02:16.550
to implemented the crown
together with formal justice

00:02:16.550 --> 00:02:19.490
mechanisms to address
consequences of violence,

00:02:19.490 --> 00:02:22.160
consequences of some
of the tendencies

00:02:22.160 --> 00:02:25.370
and incidents in Africa,
including legacies

00:02:25.370 --> 00:02:29.480
of Africa's brutal past to
ensure that we move away

00:02:29.480 --> 00:02:35.270
from that history of
brutalization and violations

00:02:35.270 --> 00:02:38.840
to economic transformation,
developmental justice,

00:02:38.840 --> 00:02:41.180
and transformative justice.

00:02:41.180 --> 00:02:43.310
LILIANE UMUBYEYI: And have
you used that framework

00:02:43.310 --> 00:02:51.620
in the context of CSVR to
tackle the colonial crimes

00:02:51.620 --> 00:02:56.450
or the legacies of colonialism
on the continent or somewhere

00:02:56.450 --> 00:02:57.563
else in the world?

00:02:57.563 --> 00:02:58.730
ANNAH MOYO: Indeed, Liliane.

00:02:58.730 --> 00:03:02.600
We are using that framework to
address a number of occurrences

00:03:02.600 --> 00:03:03.890
on the continent.

00:03:03.890 --> 00:03:05.930
If you look at a
number of countries

00:03:05.930 --> 00:03:09.410
on the African continent,
some of the conflicts

00:03:09.410 --> 00:03:11.960
that we are experiencing
currently really

00:03:11.960 --> 00:03:15.800
stem from some of the
unresolved issues of the past.

00:03:15.800 --> 00:03:18.020
The colonial era
legacies issues.

00:03:18.020 --> 00:03:20.750
Of land in Africa.

00:03:20.750 --> 00:03:25.910
That has not yet been
redistributed, for instance,

00:03:25.910 --> 00:03:30.020
from the colonial era
honors, for instance,

00:03:30.020 --> 00:03:34.220
is one of the root causes
of conflict in Africa.

00:03:34.220 --> 00:03:37.760
Issues of access to resources,
access to opportunities,

00:03:37.760 --> 00:03:39.860
for instance,
access, for instance,

00:03:39.860 --> 00:03:43.400
to development in
Africa still very much

00:03:43.400 --> 00:03:45.920
is an unresolved
issue of the past.

00:03:45.920 --> 00:03:48.860
So we are using this
framework of addressing

00:03:48.860 --> 00:03:52.340
the unresolved issues of the
past and the colonial era

00:03:52.340 --> 00:03:54.290
legacies through
transitional justice

00:03:54.290 --> 00:03:56.330
to ensure that we
deal with issues

00:03:56.330 --> 00:03:59.660
of redistributive justice
through transitional justice.

00:03:59.660 --> 00:04:01.520
We deal with the
issues, for instance,

00:04:01.520 --> 00:04:04.710
of addressing some of the
past issues, for instance,

00:04:04.710 --> 00:04:08.000
the high levels of inequality
in a number of African countries

00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:10.760
really stem from
Africa's unequal past

00:04:10.760 --> 00:04:13.820
when it comes to the
rights, the access,

00:04:13.820 --> 00:04:16.700
and opportunities that
are available for citizens

00:04:16.700 --> 00:04:18.529
vis a vis those available.

00:04:18.529 --> 00:04:23.180
For instance, for colonial
masters or colonial era owners,

00:04:23.180 --> 00:04:23.910
for instance.

00:04:23.910 --> 00:04:26.460
Those who colonized
Africa in the past.

00:04:26.460 --> 00:04:30.830
So we have an Africa,
where our colonial era

00:04:30.830 --> 00:04:33.470
honors, or colonial
era masters live

00:04:33.470 --> 00:04:38.360
alongside the majority
of Africans who are poor,

00:04:38.360 --> 00:04:42.860
who are excluded from the
mainstream economic activity

00:04:42.860 --> 00:04:46.430
because of the segregation
and the exclusionary laws

00:04:46.430 --> 00:04:47.750
during colonial era.

00:04:47.750 --> 00:04:50.360
So how do we address
some of those issues?

00:04:50.360 --> 00:04:54.260
We really need to rethink and
relook at some of the issues,

00:04:54.260 --> 00:04:58.280
for instance, the inequalities
brought about by colonialism

00:04:58.280 --> 00:05:00.260
in Africa, addressing
them as some

00:05:00.260 --> 00:05:03.020
of the root causes of
the current conflicts

00:05:03.020 --> 00:05:04.802
that we experience in Africa.

00:05:04.802 --> 00:05:07.010
Some of the tensions we see
in a number of countries,

00:05:07.010 --> 00:05:10.550
for instance, are
really need us to adopt

00:05:10.550 --> 00:05:13.160
that lens of addressing
some of these issues

00:05:13.160 --> 00:05:16.100
through transitional
justice and addressing them

00:05:16.100 --> 00:05:18.560
from a redistributive
justice lens.

00:05:18.560 --> 00:05:22.070
That is one of the elements
enshrined in the African Union

00:05:22.070 --> 00:05:25.490
Transitional Justice Policy
as an element or a pillar

00:05:25.490 --> 00:05:27.470
of transitional
justice to address

00:05:27.470 --> 00:05:32.110
some of these issues stemming
from Africa's brutal past.

00:05:32.110 --> 00:05:33.520
LILIANE UMUBYEYI:
Can you give us

00:05:33.520 --> 00:05:42.080
concrete examples
of how you are you

00:05:42.080 --> 00:05:47.270
are mobilizing this framework
with specific countries?

00:05:47.270 --> 00:05:48.680
ANNAH MOYO: Certainly.

00:05:48.680 --> 00:05:52.100
Some countries, for instance,
like Namibia are currently

00:05:52.100 --> 00:05:57.440
undertaking an audit around
the land reform agenda,

00:05:57.440 --> 00:05:58.290
for instance.

00:05:58.290 --> 00:06:00.140
So we are coming
in saying, there

00:06:00.140 --> 00:06:02.480
is a need to use
transitional justice

00:06:02.480 --> 00:06:04.280
lens to that discussion.

00:06:04.280 --> 00:06:10.316
It cannot be simply a matter
of land expropriation.

00:06:10.316 --> 00:06:13.460
It has to be an
issue that is looked

00:06:13.460 --> 00:06:15.170
from a transitional
justice lens,

00:06:15.170 --> 00:06:18.710
through risk redistributive
justice that I spoke about.

00:06:18.710 --> 00:06:21.560
How does it look like from
a transitional justice lens?

00:06:21.560 --> 00:06:24.380
How do we address this
as an issue of the past?

00:06:24.380 --> 00:06:28.490
The unresolved past the
colonial era legacy in Namibia,

00:06:28.490 --> 00:06:29.330
for instance.

00:06:29.330 --> 00:06:31.250
That, for instance,
is causing tensions

00:06:31.250 --> 00:06:36.420
in the current
governance in Namibia.

00:06:36.420 --> 00:06:38.930
So having a transitional
justice lens

00:06:38.930 --> 00:06:42.302
then will ensure that we look
at land ownership issues,

00:06:42.302 --> 00:06:43.760
and look at, for
instance, who were

00:06:43.760 --> 00:06:46.670
the original owners
of the land prior

00:06:46.670 --> 00:06:51.800
before colonial colonization
and seeking then

00:06:51.800 --> 00:06:55.520
to at least redistribute
land equally evenly

00:06:55.520 --> 00:06:57.680
amongst the populace of Namibia.

00:06:57.680 --> 00:07:00.020
So a transitional
justice look then

00:07:00.020 --> 00:07:02.300
forces us, not
only to look at it

00:07:02.300 --> 00:07:05.390
from an economic point of
view or from a land ownership

00:07:05.390 --> 00:07:08.570
point of view, but to really
look at who are the owners.

00:07:08.570 --> 00:07:12.080
In some instances,
colonial lands

00:07:12.080 --> 00:07:14.450
belonging to ethnic
groups was taken.

00:07:14.450 --> 00:07:16.340
So some of it
really will require

00:07:16.340 --> 00:07:19.430
that we redistribute that
land to the original owners

00:07:19.430 --> 00:07:22.730
and ensure that that
ownership of the land

00:07:22.730 --> 00:07:25.430
to the original
settlers is retained.

00:07:25.430 --> 00:07:28.850
But at the same time, addressing
the high levels of inequality

00:07:28.850 --> 00:07:31.370
in the country through
transitional justice.

00:07:31.370 --> 00:07:33.200
So with transitional
justice we are

00:07:33.200 --> 00:07:37.610
looking at addressing or
redressing some of the wrongs--

00:07:37.610 --> 00:07:39.260
the colonial era wrongs--

00:07:39.260 --> 00:07:42.080
at the same time, ensuring
that we are transforming

00:07:42.080 --> 00:07:45.200
and developing the
lives of Namibians

00:07:45.200 --> 00:07:47.450
currently in the
current dispensation,

00:07:47.450 --> 00:07:51.620
ensuring that issues of equity
equitable redistribution

00:07:51.620 --> 00:07:54.620
of resources of land
and opportunities

00:07:54.620 --> 00:07:57.500
for agriculture, for
economic development,

00:07:57.500 --> 00:08:00.620
and economic empowerment
are distributed evenly

00:08:00.620 --> 00:08:04.340
across races, across
ethnic groups,

00:08:04.340 --> 00:08:06.710
ethnicity, and across
the population,

00:08:06.710 --> 00:08:09.867
the whole population, and
the people of Namibia.

00:08:09.867 --> 00:08:11.450
LILIANE UMUBYEYI:
Thank you very much,

00:08:11.450 --> 00:08:17.610
and I guess it's very obvious
when you are on a continent.

00:08:17.610 --> 00:08:22.790
And when you can see every
day like the different ways

00:08:22.790 --> 00:08:25.010
in which colonialism
is still present

00:08:25.010 --> 00:08:27.920
and its legacy is
still structuring

00:08:27.920 --> 00:08:31.140
the lives of the population.

00:08:31.140 --> 00:08:33.890
But what about-- do
you think that we

00:08:33.890 --> 00:08:35.690
are this framework of
transitional justice

00:08:35.690 --> 00:08:40.400
could be useful in tackling
the legacies of colonialism

00:08:40.400 --> 00:08:49.670
in the global North, where maybe
states like here and in Belgium

00:08:49.670 --> 00:08:53.270
could be inclined to
say it's something

00:08:53.270 --> 00:09:02.510
which happened in the past
which belongs to the past,

00:09:02.510 --> 00:09:03.900
it's not the present anymore.

00:09:03.900 --> 00:09:07.770
So first of all, do you
think it's relevant,

00:09:07.770 --> 00:09:12.200
it could be useful, and
how could it be done?

00:09:12.200 --> 00:09:14.990
ANNAH MOYO: Thank you,
Liliane for that question.

00:09:14.990 --> 00:09:18.140
Issues of the past
can never really

00:09:18.140 --> 00:09:20.120
be buried with
the past, which is

00:09:20.120 --> 00:09:26.690
why, for instance, many
former colonial countries will

00:09:26.690 --> 00:09:28.340
be pushing for to
say it's in the past,

00:09:28.340 --> 00:09:30.410
let's let bygones be bygones.

00:09:30.410 --> 00:09:33.380
Let's move forward,
but really, the past--

00:09:33.380 --> 00:09:35.870
at least in the African
continent-- continues

00:09:35.870 --> 00:09:40.670
to haunt the present and it
also threatens the future,

00:09:40.670 --> 00:09:43.130
for instance, because
some of those legacies

00:09:43.130 --> 00:09:46.400
continue to be to
manifest themselves

00:09:46.400 --> 00:09:48.500
as root causes of
current conflicts

00:09:48.500 --> 00:09:52.220
and current manifestations
of tensions that

00:09:52.220 --> 00:09:54.950
lead to conflict that can easily
lead to gross human rights

00:09:54.950 --> 00:09:55.890
violations.

00:09:55.890 --> 00:09:58.730
So definitely, we need
to revisit the past.

00:09:58.730 --> 00:10:04.130
If you look at a country like
Germany, for instance, in 2018,

00:10:04.130 --> 00:10:07.490
Germany developed a
transition adjusted strategy

00:10:07.490 --> 00:10:09.860
to deal with the
past, especially

00:10:09.860 --> 00:10:11.750
in the African
countries that were

00:10:11.750 --> 00:10:14.240
former colonies of Germany.

00:10:14.240 --> 00:10:19.160
Again, this was an
acknowledgment by Germany

00:10:19.160 --> 00:10:22.010
of the fact that we cannot
really bury the past.

00:10:22.010 --> 00:10:24.140
The past is very much present.

00:10:24.140 --> 00:10:27.710
The only way we can move
forward and really begin

00:10:27.710 --> 00:10:29.360
the development--

00:10:29.360 --> 00:10:33.110
begin to give developmental
aid to some of these countries

00:10:33.110 --> 00:10:35.990
is really by first and
foremost addressing the past,

00:10:35.990 --> 00:10:39.770
because even though we may
say we are moving forward

00:10:39.770 --> 00:10:42.320
collectively, but
the very communities,

00:10:42.320 --> 00:10:45.320
the ethnic groups that
were affected by genocide,

00:10:45.320 --> 00:10:48.140
gross human rights
violations are slavery

00:10:48.140 --> 00:10:51.710
and many other
international crimes

00:10:51.710 --> 00:10:53.810
that happened during
the colonial era

00:10:53.810 --> 00:10:55.760
are very much wounded.

00:10:55.760 --> 00:10:59.120
They are still a wounded.

00:10:59.120 --> 00:11:03.030
The past is not in the past,
but still very much alive.

00:11:03.030 --> 00:11:06.500
The experiences have been
passed on through generations

00:11:06.500 --> 00:11:09.560
through narration oral
history you name it.

00:11:09.560 --> 00:11:14.150
So communities are aware of what
happened, and therefore, when

00:11:14.150 --> 00:11:18.020
nothing is being seen to be
done to address or redress

00:11:18.020 --> 00:11:21.590
colonial era wrongs
or legacies or crimes,

00:11:21.590 --> 00:11:25.350
then this fuels tensions
amongst those communities,

00:11:25.350 --> 00:11:31.970
especially where the very
same colonial era masters come

00:11:31.970 --> 00:11:34.370
in the name of
providing aid, providing

00:11:34.370 --> 00:11:38.960
support and developmental
and economic support

00:11:38.960 --> 00:11:44.060
to the very countries that they
previously committed atrocities

00:11:44.060 --> 00:11:45.590
and wrongs to.

00:11:45.590 --> 00:11:49.130
So that is the fuels that
tension, especially when

00:11:49.130 --> 00:11:52.640
now you look at other
tensions, for instance,

00:11:52.640 --> 00:11:55.490
in other fault
lines of ethnicity,

00:11:55.490 --> 00:12:01.670
fault lines of our political
party, and association

00:12:01.670 --> 00:12:02.620
amongst others.

00:12:02.620 --> 00:12:07.190
So inasmuch as if a particular
president in the global South

00:12:07.190 --> 00:12:10.370
may accept that aid, but
those who are aggrieved

00:12:10.370 --> 00:12:12.200
or those who are
affected, if they belong

00:12:12.200 --> 00:12:14.780
to the opposite ethnic
group, for instance,

00:12:14.780 --> 00:12:19.230
this tends to fuel tensions
amongst community members.

00:12:19.230 --> 00:12:23.190
And we have seen some of
these tensions becoming

00:12:23.190 --> 00:12:26.450
a full blown conflict
or full blown wars

00:12:26.450 --> 00:12:28.280
in some of the
countries in Africa.

00:12:28.280 --> 00:12:29.810
Look at Angola, for instance.

00:12:29.810 --> 00:12:34.040
Look at countries, for instance,
that are still now battling

00:12:34.040 --> 00:12:38.360
with a lot of with a lot
of instability like Guinea

00:12:38.360 --> 00:12:40.100
that just had a coup recently.

00:12:40.100 --> 00:12:41.600
So these are some
of the fault lines

00:12:41.600 --> 00:12:43.910
that usually when we
don't address the past,

00:12:43.910 --> 00:12:46.160
then the past continues
to haunt the present

00:12:46.160 --> 00:12:48.920
and it threatens the
stability of the future

00:12:48.920 --> 00:12:52.820
and the sustainable peace
that we try to build

00:12:52.820 --> 00:12:54.150
for future generations.

00:12:54.150 --> 00:12:56.720
So there is a need to
address the past in order

00:12:56.720 --> 00:12:59.840
to secure the
present and secure,

00:12:59.840 --> 00:13:03.620
again, a future that is
free of violence and also

00:13:03.620 --> 00:13:08.960
that is transformational and
leads to sustainable peace.

00:13:08.960 --> 00:13:10.890
So what I would
say, for instance,

00:13:10.890 --> 00:13:13.590
for countries in the
global North, especially

00:13:13.590 --> 00:13:15.360
European countries,

00:13:15.360 --> 00:13:18.690
Europe, for instance, developed
a transitional justice policy,

00:13:18.690 --> 00:13:21.060
but what is a big
gap in this policy

00:13:21.060 --> 00:13:23.400
is addressing
issues of the past?

00:13:23.400 --> 00:13:26.220
So we are having a policy
in the European Union

00:13:26.220 --> 00:13:30.360
that focuses on current
and future conflicts

00:13:30.360 --> 00:13:33.360
without really addressing
issues of the past.

00:13:33.360 --> 00:13:38.190
That, in itself, is a big gap,
because we cannot dissociate

00:13:38.190 --> 00:13:41.550
the past and the current
occurrences of conflict in many

00:13:41.550 --> 00:13:45.070
African countries
from the colonial era.

00:13:45.070 --> 00:13:47.790
So there is a need for
a holistic approach

00:13:47.790 --> 00:13:51.300
to addressing conflicts in
Africa that really go back

00:13:51.300 --> 00:13:52.200
to the past.

00:13:52.200 --> 00:13:55.800
The colonial era wrongs,
the colonial era legacies

00:13:55.800 --> 00:13:58.200
that has been
unresolved until now.

00:13:58.200 --> 00:14:00.270
And starting from
there, in order

00:14:00.270 --> 00:14:06.450
to build a culture of
non-punity for those

00:14:06.450 --> 00:14:08.100
who committed those
wrongs at the time,

00:14:08.100 --> 00:14:13.780
but also for future perpetrators
of these crimes in the future,

00:14:13.780 --> 00:14:16.110
whether they are
Africans or non Africans.

00:14:16.110 --> 00:14:18.390
And we are seeing a
lot of proliferation,

00:14:18.390 --> 00:14:21.870
for instance, of guns
coming from Europe, coming

00:14:21.870 --> 00:14:25.230
from the global
North, for instance.

00:14:25.230 --> 00:14:28.440
So even the current
conflicts, for instance,

00:14:28.440 --> 00:14:31.830
do have a starting
point, they do

00:14:31.830 --> 00:14:34.060
have instigators and fuelers.

00:14:34.060 --> 00:14:37.050
So the wrong of the global
North can never really

00:14:37.050 --> 00:14:41.910
be overlooked in the
current conflicts.

00:14:41.910 --> 00:14:44.490
And addressing some
of these really

00:14:44.490 --> 00:14:47.970
has to go back to the history,
go back to the colonial era

00:14:47.970 --> 00:14:49.890
wrongs that were
committed and start

00:14:49.890 --> 00:14:53.700
addressing them coming from the
past, coming to the present,

00:14:53.700 --> 00:14:57.420
and then seeking also
to secure and build

00:14:57.420 --> 00:15:01.740
a transformative,
sustainable, peaceful future.

00:15:01.740 --> 00:15:03.880
LILIANE UMUBYEYI: Before--
thank you very much.

00:15:03.880 --> 00:15:08.880
It's fascinating.

00:15:08.880 --> 00:15:11.880
Before we start
this recording, we

00:15:11.880 --> 00:15:16.020
were talking about the
technical nightmares

00:15:16.020 --> 00:15:24.330
or technical
nightmare that we face

00:15:24.330 --> 00:15:30.660
when we bring those crimes, the
colonial crimes into courts.

00:15:34.530 --> 00:15:40.830
So what are the constraints of
using the transitional justice

00:15:40.830 --> 00:15:47.070
framework, because as you or you
have said with many advantages

00:15:47.070 --> 00:15:54.580
related to the fact that we can
tackle the roots of conflicts.

00:15:54.580 --> 00:15:56.790
So I think in terms
of proscription,

00:15:56.790 --> 00:15:58.020
we have more advantages.

00:15:58.020 --> 00:15:59.670
Maybe you can develop
on that point,

00:15:59.670 --> 00:16:01.380
but what are the constraints?

00:16:06.210 --> 00:16:08.610
To what extent is
it really the way

00:16:08.610 --> 00:16:13.020
through which people
have to frame or to shape

00:16:13.020 --> 00:16:14.040
their struggles?

00:16:16.640 --> 00:16:19.370
ANNAH MOYO: Thank you,
Liliane, for that question.

00:16:19.370 --> 00:16:23.480
Indeed, I have alluded
to some of the advantages

00:16:23.480 --> 00:16:26.720
and some of the opportunities
for transitional justice

00:16:26.720 --> 00:16:27.740
to address this.

00:16:27.740 --> 00:16:30.140
One of the biggest
opportunities I

00:16:30.140 --> 00:16:32.810
think just before I go
into the constraints.

00:16:32.810 --> 00:16:35.030
One of the opportunities
of transitional justice

00:16:35.030 --> 00:16:37.100
is that there is a recognition.

00:16:37.100 --> 00:16:38.690
For instance, when
we are dealing

00:16:38.690 --> 00:16:40.940
with these issues through
transitional justice

00:16:40.940 --> 00:16:43.790
that we are dealing with
an abnormal situation, that

00:16:43.790 --> 00:16:47.180
therefore calls for
some form of flexibility

00:16:47.180 --> 00:16:52.880
in terms of really looking
at the narrower and very

00:16:52.880 --> 00:17:00.620
restrictive international law
and international human rights

00:17:00.620 --> 00:17:04.910
tenets that seek to
identify a perpetrator,

00:17:04.910 --> 00:17:09.410
seek to identify a victim,
and therefore, the perpetrator

00:17:09.410 --> 00:17:13.819
must be brought to book and
the victim must be redressed,

00:17:13.819 --> 00:17:15.290
but there must be reparation.

00:17:15.290 --> 00:17:18.290
But within a transitional
justice, the higher number

00:17:18.290 --> 00:17:21.380
of trade-offs built
within the system,

00:17:21.380 --> 00:17:24.290
for instance, yes, perpetrators
have to be identified.

00:17:24.290 --> 00:17:26.660
And there has to be
acknowledgment of having

00:17:26.660 --> 00:17:27.920
committed some wrongs.

00:17:27.920 --> 00:17:29.990
But at the same time,
the kind of justice

00:17:29.990 --> 00:17:33.680
that we are talking about
is not perfect justice,

00:17:33.680 --> 00:17:37.400
it is justice that comes with
a number of trade-offs, where,

00:17:37.400 --> 00:17:41.840
for instance, sometimes
an apology can be taken

00:17:41.840 --> 00:17:44.300
is what is needed to
redress, for instance,

00:17:44.300 --> 00:17:47.600
or to satisfy those who
are victims reparations,

00:17:47.600 --> 00:17:51.440
not in the true sense of
the full measure of what

00:17:51.440 --> 00:17:53.690
the victim suffered,
but as a token

00:17:53.690 --> 00:17:57.290
or as a symbol of
acknowledgment.

00:17:57.290 --> 00:17:59.930
So symbolic reparations
are some of the trade-offs,

00:17:59.930 --> 00:18:00.540
for instance.

00:18:00.540 --> 00:18:03.050
So it's not your
perfect justice,

00:18:03.050 --> 00:18:05.360
but it's justice that
comes with trade-offs,

00:18:05.360 --> 00:18:08.030
but that also acknowledges
the experiences

00:18:08.030 --> 00:18:11.450
of those who suffered and
also the perpetration of those

00:18:11.450 --> 00:18:13.970
who may have perpetrated
the atrocities

00:18:13.970 --> 00:18:17.150
and those coming
together coexisting

00:18:17.150 --> 00:18:20.330
towards moving forward, being
transformed, transforming

00:18:20.330 --> 00:18:23.750
perpetrators, and victims
into active citizens who

00:18:23.750 --> 00:18:25.220
can live alongside each other.

00:18:25.220 --> 00:18:29.360
Who can partner
together in order

00:18:29.360 --> 00:18:35.040
to build a sustainable,
peaceful future.

00:18:35.040 --> 00:18:37.880
So some of the constraints
of transitional justice, once

00:18:37.880 --> 00:18:42.590
again, is the fact that
there may not be political

00:18:42.590 --> 00:18:45.170
will, in most
instances, from those

00:18:45.170 --> 00:18:47.150
who are seen to be
perpetrators, especially

00:18:47.150 --> 00:18:51.200
for colonial era wrongs.

00:18:51.200 --> 00:18:53.990
The states who may have
perpetrated those atrocities

00:18:53.990 --> 00:18:57.290
may not be willing
to reopen the chapter

00:18:57.290 --> 00:19:02.300
to look at what happened, begin
a process of truth seeking,

00:19:02.300 --> 00:19:05.240
then identification of
perpetrators, acknowledging

00:19:05.240 --> 00:19:07.790
that indeed wrongs
were committed

00:19:07.790 --> 00:19:12.170
and seeking to forge a
path together with victims

00:19:12.170 --> 00:19:14.840
towards addressing some
of these atrocities

00:19:14.840 --> 00:19:18.980
and together achieving,
or rather building

00:19:18.980 --> 00:19:23.040
towards achieving sustainable
peace in the long term.

00:19:23.040 --> 00:19:26.900
So there might not be any
political will whatsoever.

00:19:26.900 --> 00:19:30.500
And transitional justice really
relies on the political will

00:19:30.500 --> 00:19:33.380
and willingness of
those who perpetrated

00:19:33.380 --> 00:19:38.390
those atrocities and the
victim, or rather the countries

00:19:38.390 --> 00:19:40.940
to whom these wrongs
were committed against.

00:19:40.940 --> 00:19:42.830
So that is the starting point.

00:19:42.830 --> 00:19:46.100
If this is not there, then
any transitional justice talks

00:19:46.100 --> 00:19:48.680
will definitely fail,
because both parties

00:19:48.680 --> 00:19:52.700
need to come to the table, need
to initiate that conversation,

00:19:52.700 --> 00:19:55.190
and map out a
transitional justice

00:19:55.190 --> 00:19:58.320
strategy on how this
will be addressed.

00:19:58.320 --> 00:20:00.080
So that is number
one constraint,

00:20:00.080 --> 00:20:02.570
and this is where,
in most countries,

00:20:02.570 --> 00:20:06.110
even countries just where
without in the global South,

00:20:06.110 --> 00:20:12.030
where there global
North perpetrator,

00:20:12.030 --> 00:20:14.870
this is how many transitional
justice processes

00:20:14.870 --> 00:20:18.050
fail because of that
lack of political will.

00:20:18.050 --> 00:20:21.950
Another constraint that I can
bring with transitional justice

00:20:21.950 --> 00:20:28.077
once again is just the
issue around records.

00:20:28.077 --> 00:20:29.660
Because now, we are
talking about what

00:20:29.660 --> 00:20:31.310
happened in the past.

00:20:31.310 --> 00:20:33.950
Colonial era in many
African countries

00:20:33.950 --> 00:20:37.820
means more than 50 years
of these atrocities

00:20:37.820 --> 00:20:38.930
having been committed.

00:20:38.930 --> 00:20:40.940
Some are committed in the 1900s.

00:20:40.940 --> 00:20:45.530
We are in 2021, so the direct
victims have passed away.

00:20:45.530 --> 00:20:49.350
So there was no record
keeping at the time.

00:20:49.350 --> 00:20:52.640
So issues of record
keeping to really determine

00:20:52.640 --> 00:20:55.160
who was a victim,
how they suffered

00:20:55.160 --> 00:20:56.310
and all of those things.

00:20:56.310 --> 00:20:59.630
So archives are not
available in many countries.

00:20:59.630 --> 00:21:03.560
So really it's a word
of mouth or experiences

00:21:03.560 --> 00:21:08.330
of victims passed on
orally by word of mouth

00:21:08.330 --> 00:21:09.600
through generations.

00:21:09.600 --> 00:21:14.120
So the first-hand narrative of
what happened may not exist.

00:21:14.120 --> 00:21:16.140
Some countries have
really done well

00:21:16.140 --> 00:21:18.570
in terms of archiving
what happened

00:21:18.570 --> 00:21:23.370
or keeping a national collective
of what happened in the past,

00:21:23.370 --> 00:21:26.230
but in many instances
for transitional justice,

00:21:26.230 --> 00:21:28.920
especially when we are
looking at reparations,

00:21:28.920 --> 00:21:31.980
a direct or rather
individual reparations,

00:21:31.980 --> 00:21:33.960
this may not be
enough, for instance,

00:21:33.960 --> 00:21:39.540
to secure individual
reparations for direct victims.

00:21:39.540 --> 00:21:41.190
It may not be
enough for instance

00:21:41.190 --> 00:21:45.120
to secure a prosecution for
those who may have committed

00:21:45.120 --> 00:21:46.560
those atrocities.

00:21:46.560 --> 00:21:49.980
But of course, other
international law principles

00:21:49.980 --> 00:21:53.370
may kick in that will really
help transitional justice

00:21:53.370 --> 00:21:54.340
processes.

00:21:54.340 --> 00:21:56.640
For instance, the
International law principle

00:21:56.640 --> 00:22:01.410
in international human rights
that even in instances where

00:22:01.410 --> 00:22:03.840
those who committed
atrocities may not

00:22:03.840 --> 00:22:10.410
be in government at the time
but the most current governments

00:22:10.410 --> 00:22:12.510
would belong to
that specific state

00:22:12.510 --> 00:22:14.970
inherit the crimes committed
by the previous government

00:22:14.970 --> 00:22:17.130
so they can still
be held accountable.

00:22:17.130 --> 00:22:19.440
They can still be
taken to court.

00:22:19.440 --> 00:22:25.050
They can still be called
to answer or respond

00:22:25.050 --> 00:22:27.720
to some of the questions
that may come up

00:22:27.720 --> 00:22:30.720
around transitional justice
reparations and so forth

00:22:30.720 --> 00:22:33.790
and they can make an apology.

00:22:33.790 --> 00:22:37.110
They can pay
reparations to victims.

00:22:37.110 --> 00:22:39.180
They can make
symbolic reparations.

00:22:39.180 --> 00:22:41.490
They can come to
the party in terms

00:22:41.490 --> 00:22:44.190
of acknowledging and
admitting the wrongs that

00:22:44.190 --> 00:22:45.607
were committed in the past.

00:22:45.607 --> 00:22:47.190
LILIANE UMUBYEYI:
Thank you very much.

00:22:47.190 --> 00:22:49.200
It's very clear.

00:22:49.200 --> 00:22:51.420
You mentioned at
some point that were

00:22:51.420 --> 00:22:55.170
building a transitional justice
strategy with the African

00:22:55.170 --> 00:22:56.100
Union.

00:22:56.100 --> 00:22:57.600
So do you think it's more--

00:23:00.180 --> 00:23:05.100
I mean on the issues of
colonial crimes more relevant

00:23:05.100 --> 00:23:07.800
from the perspective
of African countries

00:23:07.800 --> 00:23:10.680
to work at the regional
level rather than

00:23:10.680 --> 00:23:12.840
at the national level?

00:23:15.690 --> 00:23:21.570
Yeah, and how this strategic,
AU strategy, African Union,

00:23:21.570 --> 00:23:26.393
is going to be deployed,
implement, and implemented?

00:23:26.393 --> 00:23:28.060
ANNAH MOYO: Thank you
for that question.

00:23:28.060 --> 00:23:30.840
So the African Union
developed the policy.

00:23:30.840 --> 00:23:35.220
It was adopted in 2019, and one
of the issues that really it

00:23:35.220 --> 00:23:38.880
focuses on is addressing
the colonial era

00:23:38.880 --> 00:23:41.220
legacies in Africa.

00:23:41.220 --> 00:23:45.210
And of course, the
policy is continental,

00:23:45.210 --> 00:23:48.750
but it's still applying
at a national level,

00:23:48.750 --> 00:23:53.020
for instance, for those
countries that are affected.

00:23:53.020 --> 00:23:55.680
So the policy really
is a guideline

00:23:55.680 --> 00:23:59.430
for member states undertaking
transitional justice processes.

00:23:59.430 --> 00:24:04.080
So it provides a menu of
transitional justice options

00:24:04.080 --> 00:24:07.230
that a country that is
undertaking a TJ process

00:24:07.230 --> 00:24:09.360
can choose from in
order to address

00:24:09.360 --> 00:24:12.180
the consequences
of past violations,

00:24:12.180 --> 00:24:14.560
past and present violations.

00:24:14.560 --> 00:24:18.990
So the application is still
very much at a national level,

00:24:18.990 --> 00:24:24.330
although the strategy really
is applicable and exist

00:24:24.330 --> 00:24:25.740
at the continental level.

00:24:25.740 --> 00:24:30.210
So really, it's still very much
obliged at continental level.

00:24:30.210 --> 00:24:33.120
But there are
definitely alternatives

00:24:33.120 --> 00:24:36.270
of applying it regionally,
especially where some

00:24:36.270 --> 00:24:38.730
of conflicts take
a regional turn

00:24:38.730 --> 00:24:42.820
or take a regional
manifestation, for instance.

00:24:42.820 --> 00:24:46.380
So those opportunities
exist, and in addressing

00:24:46.380 --> 00:24:49.440
some of the regional concerns
or regional manifestations

00:24:49.440 --> 00:24:53.250
of conflict are some of the
regional economic communities

00:24:53.250 --> 00:24:57.090
are also now engaging taking
that continental transitional

00:24:57.090 --> 00:25:00.870
justice policy and
developing their own regional

00:25:00.870 --> 00:25:04.530
region-specific transitional
justice policies

00:25:04.530 --> 00:25:07.710
or strategies to address some
of the regional contextual

00:25:07.710 --> 00:25:08.610
realities.

00:25:08.610 --> 00:25:10.470
So it can apply regionally.

00:25:10.470 --> 00:25:15.210
It can also apply nationally,
but we are seeing a trend now,

00:25:15.210 --> 00:25:18.780
where there is a regional
strategy that really seeks

00:25:18.780 --> 00:25:21.090
to address some of
the regional realities

00:25:21.090 --> 00:25:22.890
and experiences of conflict.

00:25:22.890 --> 00:25:26.760
As we see with some of these
issues like violent extremism,

00:25:26.760 --> 00:25:29.530
there is the regional
connotation to them.

00:25:29.530 --> 00:25:31.380
So a regional
approach is much more

00:25:31.380 --> 00:25:34.140
preferred than a national
approach that may not really

00:25:34.140 --> 00:25:36.720
address or redress
the issues that

00:25:36.720 --> 00:25:39.390
affect a specific region
or a number of countries

00:25:39.390 --> 00:25:40.707
within a region.

00:25:40.707 --> 00:25:42.290
LILIANE UMUBYEYI:
Thank you very much,

00:25:42.290 --> 00:25:47.370
and I'm looking forward to
having a discussion with you

00:25:47.370 --> 00:25:51.660
and the students and activists,
because I think you are you

00:25:51.660 --> 00:25:57.450
bring a number of very
important elements

00:25:57.450 --> 00:26:03.930
that we have to consider
when we address or think

00:26:03.930 --> 00:26:08.880
about the possibility
of pursuing justice

00:26:08.880 --> 00:26:14.480
for colonial crimes in
the context of Belgium.

